### 1. Virtual machine installation loop

- **What happened:** As mentioned in the README, before installing Fedora on
  my physical machine, I practised the installation process using
  VirtualBox, to get familiar with the procedure and reduce the risk of
  damaging my existing Windows install. During one of those practice runs,
  the virtual machine restarted after Fedora finished installing, but
  instead of booting into the newly installed OS, it returned to the
  installer again, creating a continuous installation loop.
- **Cause:** VirtualBox's boot order still had the virtual optical drive
  (the installation media) prioritised above the virtual hard disk, so every
  restart re-launched the installer instead of booting the new system.
- **Fix:** I changed the virtual machine's boot order in VirtualBox:
  1. Open the virtual machine's **Settings**.
  2. Navigate to **System > Motherboard**.
  3. Locate the **Boot Order** list.
  4. Make sure **Hard Disk** is enabled, and move it above **Optical** using
     the up and down arrows.

  This prioritised the virtual hard disk over the installation media, so
  VirtualBox booted into the newly installed Fedora system instead of
  restarting the installer.

Apart from this VirtualBox issue, the installation and setup of Fedora on the
real machine went very smoothly, with no added problems, and it made my
computer feel noticeably smoother and cleaner overall.

### 2. Fedora picked as the default boot priority

- **What happened:** Not really a bug, but it did cause complications with
  Windows. Whenever my laptop turned on, Fedora was chosen as the boot
  priority by default, so unless I interrupted the boot with F12 and
  selected Windows manually, it would automatically boot into Fedora. This
  was actually intentional on my part, since I wanted Fedora to boot first
  and push me into daily-driving it instead of Windows. The complication came
  during Windows updates: when Windows restarted mid-update, it booted into
  Fedora instead of continuing the update.
- **Cause:** Fedora's bootloader (GRUB) was set as the default boot entry,
  which doesn't distinguish between a normal restart and a Windows-update
  restart, so it always sent the system to Fedora unless I intervened.
- **Fix:** Changing the boot priority to Windows during updates, then back to
  Fedora afterward, would have been a cleaner fix, but what I actually did
  was give the screen my full attention during updates, and manually
  interrupt the boot to select Windows whenever a restart happened.

### 3. Switching to Windows from Fedora triggered a broken BitLocker prompt

- **What happened:** Whenever I missed the boot interruption and ended up
  selecting Windows from GRUB's own boot menu (rather than Windows Boot
  Manager directly from F12), it showed a BitLocker recovery screen.
  Entering the recovery code didn't work, and I had to hard reset, boot back
  into Fedora, interrupt the boot again, and select Windows from the
  firmware's boot menu instead.
- **Cause:** This turned out to be a known GRUB + BitLocker + TPM issue.
  When GRUB chainloads into Windows (rather than Windows being booted
  directly by the firmware), it changes the TPM's boot measurements, so
  BitLocker can't confirm the boot path is safe and falls back to asking for
  the recovery key. Going through F12 and choosing Windows Boot Manager
  directly at the firmware level skips GRUB entirely, which is why that path
  worked and GRUB's own menu didn't.
- **Fix:** I never found a permanent fix, just the workaround of always
  using F12 to pick Windows directly. Two real fixes exist, documented by
  others who've hit the same issue, though I didn't try them myself:
  1. Edit GRUB's Windows boot entry to exit cleanly before chainloading
     (using a tool like Grub Customizer), so Windows boots in a way BitLocker
     recognises.
  2. Properly re-enable Secure Boot for the Linux side (for example with
     `sbctl`), since BitLocker's TPM protector expects Secure Boot to be on
     to unseal the key safely.
- **Lesson:** If you're dual-booting with BitLocker, always boot Windows
  directly from the firmware's boot menu (F12 or equivalent), not from
  inside GRUB's own menu, unless you've specifically fixed GRUB's handoff
  first.

### 4. Safeblock set the shared SSD to read-only

- **What happened:** While working on one of my assignments, I noticed I
  couldn't open the shared SSD or view and use its contents.
- **Cause:** Safeblock converted the SSD to read-only mode, which stopped
  Fedora from making any changes to the files on it.
- **Fix:** I wasn't able to fix this either. See
  [leaving-fedora.md](leaving-fedora.md) for the full story and everything I
  tried.

